Hi,

I am investigating model misspecification in mirt.
I generated lots of sets of data using one dimension (one random effect) then fit each of dataset using a two-dimensional model. Why none of the estimated correlation hits the true value in my simulation (the highest estimated cor between two dimensions is .93 instead of the true value 1)?
Any tips?

Thanks,
FJ

From ub330c2028fed415b at groups.google.com  Sun Oct 21 14:09:15 2018
From: ub330c2028fed415b at groups.google.com (Phil Chalmers)
Date: Sun, 21 Oct 2018 14:09:15 +0000
Subject: Re: Perfect correlation between two dimensions
Message-ID: <99E7K1_s30g.JrbSqoJRAwAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <99E7K1_s30g.EMwmbxUpBwAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
References: <99E7K1_s30g.EMwmbxUpBwAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/mirt-package/c/99E7K1_s30g/m/JrbSqoJRAwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Likelihood space for correlations is rather curved, so getting correlations near 1 is much more difficult. Actually obtaining a value of 1 would also crash the estimation algorithm because the covariance matrix would not be invertable.

Phil

